-- The site owner's notes on the FAQ answers ("A note from the site owner"), managed on the Drupal "FAQ notes"
-- page (scripts/manage_faq_notes.py, faq_notes.py). A note belongs to one question, by its wording, like the
-- stored answers (faq_answers). The body is HTML cleaned with the allowlist in faq_notes.py.
--
-- The table is made, and the owner's eight notes (written in frontend/src/FaqOwnerNote.tsx until 2026-11-10)
-- copied into it, only when it does not exist yet. Every migration is run again at each deploy and container
-- start, so a note the owner deletes stays deleted. Like users and faq_answers it is not in schema.sql, so
-- re-applying that file cannot wipe it.
--
-- Apply with:
--   psql -d bibleandbeyond -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f database/migrations/021_add_faq_notes.sql
-- (locally: docker exec -i bible-and-beyond-database psql -U bibleandbeyond -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 < database/migrations/021_add_faq_notes.sql)

DO $migration$
BEGIN
  IF to_regclass('public.faq_notes') IS NULL THEN
    CREATE TABLE faq_notes (
      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
      question TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
      body TEXT NOT NULL,
      created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
      updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
    );

    INSERT INTO faq_notes (question, body) VALUES
      ($note$How should Christians understand LGBTQ identities and relationships?$note$,
       $note$<p>This is a very controversial subject, and I’m sure I am going to make many people angry. In my opinion, too much emphasis has been placed on sexual sin.</p><blockquote><p>“For all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, and are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=45&amp;chapter=3&amp;verse=23" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Romans 3:23–24 (opens in a new tab)">Romans 3:23–24</a></p></footer></blockquote><blockquote><p>“Do not judge, and you will not be judged. Do not condemn, and you will not be condemned. Forgive, and you will be forgiven.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=42&amp;chapter=6&amp;verse=37" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Luke 6:37 (opens in a new tab)">Luke 6:37</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>We are told not to judge. The Greek word for sin is <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=45&amp;chapter=3&amp;verse=23&amp;strong=G0264" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="ἁμαρτάνω, hamartanō, in the Greek lexicon (opens in a new tab)"><strong lang="grc">ἁμαρτάνω</strong> (<em>hamartanō</em>)</a>: “to sin, to miss the mark; hence, to err, to do wrong.” The term comes from archery and means that you missed the target. It doesn’t matter whether you miss by inches or by yards. We all sin daily.</p><p>Jesus set a high bar for sin:</p><blockquote><p>“Now I tell you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman commits adultery.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=19&amp;verse=9" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 19:9 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 19:9</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>Most Protestants no longer have a problem with divorce. I have many good Christian friends who have been divorced and remarried.</p><p>Here is another:</p><blockquote><p>“But I tell you that anyone who is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Again, anyone who says to his brother, ‘Raca,’ will be subject to the Sanhedrin. But anyone who says, ‘You fool!’ will be subject to the fire of hell.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=5&amp;verse=22" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 5:22 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 5:22</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>So it is evident that we all sin. How, then, can anyone be saved? By grace (see the <a href="/faq?question=Is%20a%20Christian%20saved%20by%20grace%20alone%3F">FAQ on grace</a>). Grace overcomes the law. I don’t know about you, but I love pork, lobster and crab, all of which are forbidden in the Old Testament. As Paul says:</p><blockquote><p>“Therefore, there is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus. For in Christ Jesus the law of the Spirit of life set you free from the law of sin and death. For what the law was powerless to do in that it was weakened by the flesh, God did by sending His own Son in the likeness of sinful man, as an offering for sin. He thus condemned sin in the flesh, so that the righteous standard of the law might be fulfilled in us, who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=45&amp;chapter=8&amp;verse=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Romans 8:1–4 (opens in a new tab)">Romans 8:1–4</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>I also have a few gay and lesbian Christian friends. As Paul said:</p><blockquote><p>“But for this very reason I was shown mercy, so that in me, the worst of sinners, Christ Jesus might display His perfect patience as an example to those who would believe in Him for eternal life.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=54&amp;chapter=1&amp;verse=16" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="1 Timothy 1:16 (opens in a new tab)">1 Timothy 1:16</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>I feel the same way as the Apostle Paul (and those of you who know me know this).</p><p>I love the motto of my church: <em>“In essential things, unity; in non-essential things, liberty; in all things, grace.”</em> So for me, if my brother or sister is divorced, gay or lesbian, addicted to alcohol or gambling, or struggling with anything else, it doesn’t matter. They are my brother or sister, saved by grace, and I love them.</p><p>If anyone is struggling with a hurt, habit or hang-up, I highly recommend <a href="https://celebraterecovery.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Celebrate Recovery (opens its website in a new tab)">Celebrate Recovery</a>. It is a Christian-based recovery program. It is not limited to addiction; it covers many other issues: depression, grief, divorce, codependency, sexual addiction, anger issues and many more.</p><blockquote><p>“Teacher, which commandment is the greatest in the Law?” Jesus declared, “‘Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.’ This is the first and greatest commandment. And the second is like it: ‘Love your neighbor as yourself.’ All the Law and the Prophets hang on these two commandments.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=22&amp;verse=36" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 22:36–40 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 22:36–40</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>Grace trumps the law. If you are following the two greatest commandments, any specific issue is irrelevant.</p>$note$),
      ($note$How can I overcome a sin I keep repeating?$note$,
       $note$<p>If you need help, <a href="https://celebraterecovery.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Celebrate Recovery (opens its website in a new tab)">Celebrate Recovery</a> may be a place to reach out to. They are there for help with your hurts, habits, or hangups. It’s not only about addictions. It can help with grief, mental health issues (depression, etc.), divorce, codependence, sexual or gambling issues and many more.</p><p>Many churches have Celebrate Recovery as one of their ministries and even if your church doesn’t, there may be a church near you that has meetings.</p><p>Personally, I help with CR at our church and I find it very useful for my hurts, habits and hangups.</p>$note$),
      ($note$How do I understand passages that seem contradictory or morally troubling?$note$,
       $note$<p>In addition to the suggestions above, you can do the following:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Check other verses</strong> (you can do this on this site in the <a href="/search">Bible Search</a>).</p><p>Sometimes you can gain insight by seeing what other writers wrote.</p></li><li><p><strong>Check other versions</strong> (you can do this on this site in the <a href="/search">Bible Search</a>).</p><p>Different versions translate passages differently.</p></li><li><p><strong>Check the original words</strong> (you can do this in the <a href="/search">Bible Search</a> or the <a href="/read">Bible Reader</a> by clicking on the word).</p><p>The Bible was originally written in Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek. Translation is a complex process and some words don’t translate directly. A classic example of a New Testament Greek word that does not have a clean, direct one-to-one equivalent in English is <em>splanchnizomai</em> (<a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=42&amp;chapter=15&amp;verse=20&amp;strong=G4697" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="σπλαγχνίζομαι, splanchnizomai, in the Greek lexicon (opens in a new tab)"><strong lang="grc">σπλαγχνίζομαι</strong></a>).</p><p>The translation problem: English Bibles usually translate this as “felt compassion” or “had pity.” However, those English words completely miss the physical and visceral weight of the original Greek. The root of the word is <em>splanchna</em>, which refers to the inward parts or upper viscera—specifically the heart, lungs, liver, and intestines (the “guts”). To the ancient Greeks and the biblical writers, these internal organs were considered the literal seat of the strongest human emotions.</p><p>Therefore, <em>splanchnizomai</em> does not just mean someone felt a mental or polite sense of sympathy. It means “to be moved in one’s bowels” or “to feel a gut-level, wrenching physical reaction” to someone else’s suffering.</p></li><li><p><strong>Consider both the cultural and Biblical context.</strong></p><p>First, read the verse in its full context. Don’t just take one verse.</p><p>Second, consider when and where it was written. When I was in Bible school, one of my professors gave this example:</p><blockquote><p>He put before them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field. Although it is the smallest of all seeds, yet it grows into the largest of garden plants and becomes a tree…”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=13&amp;verse=31" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 13:31–32 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 13:31–32</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>We know today that that is not technically true. Orchid seeds are often microscopic, but I’m fairly sure that few people at the time knew that. Jesus did (being God), but He knew His listeners wouldn’t understand if He started talking about orchids (also it would spoil the analogy).</p></li><li><p><strong>Talk to other Christians.</strong></p><p>Sometimes, others may have already dealt with this and may have some insights.</p></li></ul>$note$),
      ($note$How do I know which biblical commands still apply today?$note$,
       $note$<p>The answer is actually in the Bible:</p><blockquote><p><sup>36</sup> “Teacher, which commandment is the greatest in the Law?” <sup>37</sup> Jesus declared, “‘Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.’ <sup>38</sup> This is the first and greatest commandment. <sup>39</sup> And the second is like it: ‘Love your neighbor as yourself.’ <strong><sup><u>40</u></sup><u> All the Law and the Prophets hang on these two commandments.</u></strong>”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=22&amp;verse=36" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 22:36–40 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 22:36–40</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>So if we obey these two commandments, we are obeying the spirit of the law.</p><p>Note: The law never passed away (see <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=5&amp;verse=17" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 5:17 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 5:17</a>), but we are justified through Christ’s sacrifice. Paul notes this in <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=48&amp;chapter=5&amp;verse=18" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Galatians 5:18 (opens in a new tab)">Galatians 5:18</a>.</p><p>So if you are obeying the greatest commandments, then that is what applies today. Love is the important thing.</p><p><strong>Soapbox:</strong> If you hate, judge or condemn people, then you are not obeying these commandments. Consider that before you judge or say “God hates…” God loves everyone and hopes they all will come to salvation.</p>$note$),
      ($note$What does Christianity teach about divorce and remarriage?$note$,
       $note$<p>I address this in <a href="/faq?question=How%20should%20Christians%20understand%20LGBTQ%20identities%20and%20relationships%3F">How should Christians understand LGBTQ identities and relationships?</a>, but here is the short answer in my opinion.</p><p>Jesus set a high bar for sin:</p><blockquote><p>“Now I tell you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman commits adultery.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=19&amp;verse=9" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 19:9 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 19:9</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>Most Protestants no longer have a problem with divorce. I have many good Christian friends who have been divorced and remarried.</p><p>Here is another:</p><blockquote><p>“But I tell you that anyone who is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Again, anyone who says to his brother, ‘Raca,’ will be subject to the Sanhedrin. But anyone who says, ‘You fool!’ will be subject to the fire of hell.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=5&amp;verse=22" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 5:22 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 5:22</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>So it is evident that we all sin. How, then, can anyone be saved? By grace (see the <a href="/faq?question=Is%20a%20Christian%20saved%20by%20grace%20alone%3F">FAQ on grace</a>). Grace overcomes the law. I don’t know about you, but I love pork, lobster and crab, all of which are forbidden in the Old Testament. As Paul says:</p><blockquote><p>“Therefore, there is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus. For in Christ Jesus the law of the Spirit of life set you free from the law of sin and death. For what the law was powerless to do in that it was weakened by the flesh, God did by sending His own Son in the likeness of sinful man, as an offering for sin. He thus condemned sin in the flesh, so that the righteous standard of the law might be fulfilled in us, who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.”</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=45&amp;chapter=8&amp;verse=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Romans 8:1–4 (opens in a new tab)">Romans 8:1–4</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>I love the motto of my church: <em>“In essential things, unity; in non-essential things, liberty; in all things, grace.”</em> So for me, if my brother or sister is divorced or struggling with anything else, it doesn’t matter. They are my brother or sister, saved by grace, and I love them.</p><p>Grace trumps the law. If you are following the two greatest commandments, any specific issue is irrelevant.</p>$note$),
      ($note$How can I recover from church hurt or spiritual abuse?$note$,
       $note$<p>If this is true in your case, then I am truly sorry. Jesus welcomes all, but unfortunately Christians are human. I wish and pray that this never happens to anyone, but realize it’s not God or Jesus. It’s broken human beings that deserve our sympathy and prayer.</p><p>If you need help, <a href="https://celebraterecovery.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Celebrate Recovery (opens its website in a new tab)">Celebrate Recovery</a> may be a place to reach out to. They are there for help with your hurts, habits, or hangups.</p><p>Many churches have Celebrate Recovery as one of their ministries and even if your church doesn’t, there may be a church near you that has meetings.</p><p>If you do go to Celebrate Recovery, be honest. Express your hurt and don’t be afraid about judgement. CR is a safe place to share your hurts judgement free.</p>$note$),
      ($note$How does Christianity relate to science, evolution, and other religions?$note$,
       $note$<p>For myself, it’s not a question of how God created the universe but rather the fact that He did create the universe. I’m not going to give away my personal belief from above, but it is in the list.</p><p>As far as other religions, I have personally grown. When I was a young Christian, I considered myself a “cult” evangelist. I had all sorts of facts and evidence why the specific “cults” were wrong. I even had an original version of the Book of Mormon that I used to “prove” that it changed. I didn’t change one person’s mind.</p><p>I’ve since grown.</p><blockquote><p><sup>22</sup> Then Paul stood up in the meeting of the Areopagus and said, “Men of Athens, I see that in every way you are very religious. <sup>23</sup> For as I walked around and examined your objects of worship, I even found an altar with this inscription: TO AN UNKNOWN GOD. Therefore what you worship as something unknown, I now proclaim to you. <sup>24</sup> The God who made the world and everything in it is the Lord of heaven and earth and does not live in temples made by human hands. <sup>25</sup> Nor is He served by human hands, as if He needed anything, because He Himself gives everyone life and breath and everything else.</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=44&amp;chapter=17&amp;verse=22" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Acts 17:22–25 (opens in a new tab)">Acts 17:22–25</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>Note that Paul doesn’t start by saying “You’re wrong; there is only one true God.” He acknowledges their beliefs but goes on to gently share the truth, creating a bridge.</p><p>I have learned that love and respect go further than knowledge and rhetoric.</p><p>I belong to a group that has people of many different beliefs (including no beliefs). I let them know that I’m a Christian and I follow Jesus, but that I respect their right to believe as they choose. I feel that I’ve made more progress in getting them to open up to my beliefs than I ever did when I was a “cult” evangelist. If you respect other beliefs, they will respect yours, and that may create an opening for Christ.</p>$note$),
      ($note$What does the Bible actually say about the end times and Jesus’ return?$note$,
       $note$<p>This is a popular but very misunderstood topic.</p><p>Where I am coming from: I accepted Christ at 14 after reading Hal Lindsey’s <cite>The Late Great Planet Earth</cite>. As an early Christian, I loved end times stories and discussions. I even created a scrapbook titled “Signs Pointing to the End Times.” Like other things, as I have grown, my views have changed. I think this is the clearest passage on Jesus’ return:</p><blockquote><p><sup>36</sup> No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father. <sup>37</sup> As it was in the days of Noah, so will it be at the coming of the Son of Man. <sup>38</sup> For in the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, up to the day Noah entered the ark. <sup>39</sup> And they were oblivious until the flood came and swept them all away. So will it be at the coming of the Son of Man.</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=40&amp;chapter=24&amp;verse=36" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Matthew 24:36–39 (opens in a new tab)">Matthew 24:36–39</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>These are Jesus’ direct words. No one but God the Father knows.</p><p>And 2 Peter 3:8–9, written about 2,000 years ago:</p><blockquote><p><sup>8</sup> Beloved, do not let this one thing escape your notice: With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years are like a day. <sup>9</sup> The Lord is not slow in keeping His promise as some understand slowness, but is patient with you, not wanting anyone to perish but everyone to come to repentance.</p><footer><p>— <a href="/?read=BSB&amp;book=61&amp;chapter=3&amp;verse=8" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="2 Peter 3:8–9 (opens in a new tab)">2 Peter 3:8–9</a></p></footer></blockquote><p>My personal opinion is that I know that Jesus is coming back, but I don’t know when, so I don’t worry about it. I don’t know if He will return before I die (I’m 68 years old) or if I will be resurrected when He comes back long in the future.</p><p>I think too much speculation on this is not a beneficial use of our time. He’s coming back; that’s what’s important.</p>$note$);
  END IF;
END
$migration$;
